@[disk-mount step]
Mount the new system's filesystems under the installer's target directory,
@[$ENCHANT_TARGET] (root first, then anything below it):
  @[mount /dev/sda2 $ENCHANT_TARGET]

UEFI: mount the EFI system partition in the target, usually at /boot/efi:
  @[mkdir -p $ENCHANT_TARGET/boot/efi]
  @[mount /dev/sda1 $ENCHANT_TARGET/boot/efi]
or at /boot (then your kernels are stored on it, ~15 MiB each). GRUB and
rEFInd work with either. Limine and systemd-boot only read FAT partitions, so
with them /boot is simplest (they need the kernel on the EFI partition). The
boot loaders are compared in @[todo boot-loader]; not sure? Use /boot/efi.
Whichever you pick, use the same mount point in fstab later.

Other separate filesystems go below it the same way, e.g.
  @[mkdir -p $ENCHANT_TARGET/home] and @[mount /dev/sda4 $ENCHANT_TARGET/home]

btrfs with subvolumes: see @[guide disks] for the mount commands.
Turn on swap if you made a swap partition: @[swapon /dev/sda3]
Check the result with @[lsblk] or @[findmnt -R $ENCHANT_TARGET].

Type @[next] to go to the next step.
